 \"It doesn't matter\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"After the parenthetical citation\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Incorrect MLA format.\",\n      \"Incorrect MLA format.\",\n      \"The period always comes after the citation in MLA.\",\n      \"MLA has specific rules for punctuation.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q107\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Hard\",\n    \"question\": \"What is the rhyme scheme of a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\",\n      \"ABBA ABBA CDC DCD\",\n      \"AABB CCDD EEFF GG\",\n      \"ABCD ABCD EFGH IJ\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct pattern for Shakespearean/English sonnets.\",\n      \"This is a Petrarchan sonnet pattern.\",\n      \"Not a traditional sonnet form.\",\n      \"Not a recognized sonnet pattern.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q108\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Hard\",\n    \"question\": \"Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"I love reading; especially novels.\",\n      \"The test is tomorrow; I need to study.\",\n      \"She is smart; because she reads a lot.\",\n      \"He ran quickly; to catch the bus.\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"The test is tomorrow; I need to study.\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Semicolon shouldn't separate a fragment.\",\n      \"Correctly joins two independent clauses.\",\n      \"'Because' creates a dependent clause.\",\n      \"'To catch' creates an infinitive phrase, not an independent clause.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q109\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"What is the difference between tone and mood?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Tone is the reader's feeling; mood is the author's attitude\",\n      \"They are the same thing\",\n      \"Tone is the author's attitude; mood is the reader's feeling\",\n      \"Tone is only in poetry; mood is only in prose\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Tone is the author's attitude; mood is the reader's feeling\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Reversed definitions.\",\n      \"They are distinct literary concepts.\",\n      \"Tone reflects the author's perspective; mood is the emotional atmosphere created for the reader.\",\n      \"Both apply to all literary forms.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q110\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which revision strategy is most effective for improving clarity?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Reading your work aloud\",\n      \"Checking spelling only\",\n      \"Counting words\",\n      \"Changing font size\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Reading your work aloud\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Hearing the text helps identify awkward phrasing and unclear passages.\",\n      \"Spelling is important but doesn't address clarity.\",\n      \"Word count doesn't improve clarity.\",\n      \"Formatting doesn't affect content clarity.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q111\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"How many lines are in a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"12 lines\",\n      \"14 lines\",\n      \"16 lines\",\n      \"18 lines\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"14 lines\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Incorrect. 12 lines would be too short for the traditional sonnet structure.\",\n      \"Correct! Shakespearean sonnets always have 14 lines written in iambic pentameter.\",\n      \"Incorrect. 16 lines exceeds the traditional sonnet length.\",\n      \"Incorrect. 18 lines is too long for a sonnet.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q112\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Hard\",\n    \"question\": \"What's the rhyme scheme of a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"ABBA ABBA CDC DCD\",\n      \"ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\",\n      \"AABB CCDD EEFF GG\",\n      \"ABCD ABCD EFGH IJ\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"ABAB CDCD EFEF GG\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This is the rhyme scheme of a Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet, not Shakespearean.\",\n      \"Correct! The three quatrains follow alternating rhyme (ABAB, CDCD, EFEF), ending with a rhyming couplet (GG).\",\n      \"This pattern uses only couplets, which doesn't match the Shakespearean structure.\",\n      \"This pattern has no rhyme scheme at all and isn't a traditional sonnet form.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q113\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Hard\",\n    \"question\": \"Where does the volta (turn in thought) typically occur in a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"At line 5\",\n      \"At line 9 or in the final couplet (line 13)\",\n      \"At the very beginning (line 1)\",\n      \"At line 8\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"At line 9 or in the final couplet (line 13)\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Too early. Line 5 is in the middle of the second quatrain, not a typical turning point.\",\n      \"Correct! The volta usually appears at the start of the third quatrain (line 9) or in the final couplet (line 13), shifting the poem's direction or offering resolution.\",\n      \"The poem typically establishes its theme first before turning, so line 1 is too early.\",\n      \"Line 8 is more common in Petrarchan sonnets, not Shakespearean structure.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q114\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which of the following is a recurring theme in Shakespeare's sonnets?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Space exploration and technology\",\n      \"Love, time, beauty, and mortality\",\n      \"Political revolution\",\n      \"Agricultural practices\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Love, time, beauty, and mortality\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Anachronistic. Shakespeare lived in the late 16th/early 17th century, long before space exploration.\",\n      \"Correct! Shakespeare's sonnets deeply explore universal themes of love, the passage of time, the nature of beauty, death, and existential questions.\",\n      \"While Shakespeare wrote political plays, his sonnets focus more on personal and philosophical themes.\",\n      \"Not a central theme in Shakespeare's sonnets, which focus on human emotion and experience.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q115\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"What's the primary focus of phonemic awareness?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Recognizing sentence rhythm\",\n      \"Recognizing individual sounds in words\",\n      \"Identifying prefixes and suffixes\",\n      \"Memorizing sight words\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Recognizing individual sounds in words\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Sentence rhythm relates to prosody, not phonemic awareness.\",\n      \"Correct! Phonemic awareness focuses on hearing, identifying, and manipulating individual sounds (phonemes) in spoken words.\",\n      \"Prefixes and suffixes are part of morphology, not phonemic awareness.\",\n      \"Sight word memorization is a separate literacy skill.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q116\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Hard\",\n    \"question\": \"Which skill bridges decoding and comprehension?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Vocabulary development\",\n      \"Phoneme blending\",\n      \"Sight word recognition\",\n      \"Oral fluency\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Oral fluency\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While important, vocabulary is more directly related to comprehension than bridging the two.\",\n      \"Phoneme blending is a decoding skill, not a bridge between decoding and comprehension.\",\n      \"Sight words support decoding but don't bridge to comprehension.\",\n      \"Correct! Oral fluency combines accurate decoding with expression and understanding, linking both skills.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q117\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"What stage comes before transitional literacy?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Emergent\",\n      \"Fluent\",\n      \"Early\",\n      \"Proficient\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Early\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Emergent is the first stage, coming before early, not transitional.\",\n      \"Fluent comes after transitional.\",\n      \"Correct! The progression is: Emergent → Early → Transitional → Fluent → Proficient.\",\n      \"Proficient is the final stage, after fluent.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q118\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Why is morphology instruction beneficial?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"It improves handwriting\",\n      \"It helps infer meaning from unfamiliar words\",\n      \"It improves sentence rhythm\",\n      \"It builds phonemic awareness\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"It helps infer meaning from unfamiliar words\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Morphology doesn't relate to handwriting (a motor skill).\",\n  