ctly creates and shapes tone.\",\n      \"Theme is the central idea, influenced by many elements.\",\n      \"Conflict is the struggle, not directly determined by diction.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q301\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Krashen's theory stating learners need input slightly above their level is called:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Input Hypothesis\",\n      \"Output Hypothesis\",\n      \"Monitor Model\",\n      \"Transfer Theory\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Input Hypothesis\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! The Input Hypothesis (i+1) states learners acquire language when exposed to comprehensible input just beyond their current level.\",\n      \"Output Hypothesis emphasizes language production, not input.\",\n      \"Monitor Model is Krashen's broader framework, not specifically about input levels.\",\n      \"Transfer Theory addresses L1 influence, not input complexity.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q302\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"The emotional factor that can block language learning is known as:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Critical Period\",\n      \"Affective Filter\",\n      \"Natural Order\",\n      \"Code-Switching\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Affective Filter\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Critical Period refers to optimal age for language acquisition.\",\n      \"Correct! The Affective Filter represents emotional barriers (anxiety, low motivation) that prevent input from being acquired.\",\n      \"Natural Order describes the sequence of grammar acquisition.\",\n      \"Code-Switching is alternating between languages, not an emotional barrier.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q303\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which strategy best supports students developing academic language (CALP)?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Playground talk\",\n      \"Scaffolded discussion using academic vocabulary\",\n      \"Total Physical Response\",\n      \"Peer imitation\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Scaffolded discussion using academic vocabulary\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Playground talk develops BICS, not CALP.\",\n      \"Correct! Scaffolded academic discussions with explicit vocabulary instruction build CALP.\",\n      \"TPR is effective for comprehension but doesn't target academic language specifically.\",\n      \"Peer imitation may support social language but doesn't ensure academic language development.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q304\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"A teacher gives clear examples of how to form past tense before student practice. This is:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Modeling\",\n      \"Drilling\",\n      \"Code-switching\",\n      \"Translation\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Modeling\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Modeling demonstrates correct language use before students attempt it independently.\",\n      \"Drilling involves repetitive practice, not initial demonstration.\",\n      \"Code-switching is language alternation, not instructional demonstration.\",\n      \"Translation converts meaning between languages, not demonstrating form.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q305\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Pairing language commands with movement is part of:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Silent Way\",\n      \"Direct Method\",\n      \"Total Physical Response\",\n      \"Communicative Approach\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Total Physical Response\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Silent Way minimizes teacher talk, doesn't emphasize movement.\",\n      \"Direct Method uses target language only, but doesn't focus on physical response.\",\n      \"Correct! TPR uses physical actions to reinforce language meaning and comprehension.\",\n      \"Communicative Approach emphasizes interaction, not specifically movement.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q306\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Waiting several seconds before calling on ELL students improves:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Grammar\",\n      \"Processing time\",\n      \"Pronunciation\",\n      \"Fluency\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Processing time\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Wait time doesn't directly teach grammar.\",\n      \"Correct! Wait time gives ELLs processing time to think, translate internally, and formulate responses.\",\n      \"Pronunciation requires different interventions.\",\n      \"While helpful, the primary benefit is processing time, not immediate fluency.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q307\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Basic Interpersonal Communication Skills (BICS) differ from CALP because they:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Require academic vocabulary\",\n      \"Develop more slowly\",\n      \"Are used in social settings\",\n      \"Depend on grammar rules\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Are used in social settings\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"BICS uses everyday vocabulary, not academic vocabulary.\",\n      \"BICS develops faster (1-2 years) than CALP (5-7 years).\",\n      \"Correct! BICS is conversational, context-embedded language used in social situations.\",\n      \"Both require grammar, but BICS is less formal and academic.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q308\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"When a Spanish-speaking student writes 'espectator' for 'spectator,' this reflects:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Language transfer\",\n      \"Code-switching\",\n      \"Fossilization\",\n      \"Overgeneralization\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Language transfer\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Language transfer occurs when L1 patterns influence L2 production (Spanish 'espectador' → 'espectator').\",\n      \"Code-switching is intentional alternation between languages, not an error.\",\n      \"Fossilization is persistent incorrect forms despite instruction.\",\n      \"Overgeneralization applies learned rules too broadly, not L1 influence.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q309\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Teachers can support second language acquisition most effectively by:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Focusing only on grammar\",\n      \"Providing comprehensible input and context\",\n      \"Avoiding visuals\",\n      \"Limiting peer interaction\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Providing comprehensible input and context\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Grammar focus alone doesn't ensure communicative competence.\",\n      \"Correct! Comprehensible input with rich context supports natural acquisition.\",\n      \"Visuals are essential supports for ELLs.\",\n      \"Peer interaction builds communicative competence.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q310\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Encouraging peer collaboration and discussion builds:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Morphology\",\n      \"Communicative competence\",\n      \"Syntax\",\n      \"Phonemic awareness\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Communicative competence\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Morphology is word structure, not built primarily through discussion.\",\n      \"Correct! Collaboration develops the ability to use language appropriately in social contexts.\",\n      \"Syntax benefits, but communicative competence is the broader outcome.\",\n      \"Phonemic awareness involves sound manipulation, not discussion.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q311\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"An affix attached to the beginning of a word is called a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Suffix\",\n      \"Prefix\",\n      \"Root\",\n      \"Stem\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Prefix\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Suffixes attach at the end.\",\n      \"Correct! Prefixes attach at the beginning (un-, re-, pre-).\",\n      \"Roots are the base meaning units.\",\n      \"Stems are bases to which affixes attach.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q312\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Which term means an affix added to the end of a root?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Prefix\",\n      \"Suffix\",\n      \"Inflection\",\n      \"Stem\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Suffix\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Prefixes attach at the beginning.\",\n      \"Correct! Suffixes attach at the end (-ness, -tion, -ly).\",\n      \"Inflection is a type of suffix, but suffix is the general term.\",\n      \"Stems are not affixes.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q313\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Which suffix changes a verb to a noun?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"-ize\",\n      \"-tion\",\n      \"-ful\",\n      \"-ous\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"-tion\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"-ize creates verbs from nouns/adjectives.\",\n      \"Correct! -tion creates nouns from verbs (act → action).\",\n      \"-ful creates adjectives from nouns.\",\n      \"-ous creates adjectives from nouns.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q314\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"The suffix '-ize' typically forms:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Nouns\",\n      \"Verbs\",\n      \"Adjectives\",\n      \"Adverbs\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Verbs\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Nouns are formed by -tion, -ness, -ment.\",\n      \"Correct! -ize forms verbs (modern → modernize).\",\n      \"Adjectives use -ful, -ous, -able.\",\n      \"Adverbs use -ly.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q315\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"The suffix '-ous' turns a noun into an:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Adjective\",\n      \"Verb\",\n      \"Adverb\",\n      \"Pronoun\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Adjective\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! -ous creates adjectives (danger → dangerous).\",\n      \"-ize, -en, -ify create verbs.\",\n      \"-ly typically creates adverbs.\",\n      \"Pronouns are not formed with suffixes.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q316\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which classroom activity best supports morphological awareness?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Memorizing spelling lists\",\n      \"Sorting words by affixes\",\n      \"Copying sentences\",\n      \"Reciting rhymes\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Sorting words by affixes\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Memorization doesn't build awareness of word structure.\",\n      \"Correct! Sorting by affixes helps students analyze and understand word parts.\",\n      \"Copying doesn't emphasize morphological analysis.\",\n      \"Rhymes support phonological awareness, not morphological.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q317\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which morpheme type changes word meaning but not grammatical role?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Inflectional\",\n      \"Derivational\",\n      \"Compound\",\n      \"Bound root\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Derivational\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Inflectional morphemes indicate grammar (tense, number) without changing meaning.\",\n      \"Correct! Derivational morphemes create new words and meanings (happy → happiness).\",\n      \"Compounds combine two words, not morphemes.\",\n      \"Bound roots don't change meaning, they carry core meaning.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q318\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Adding '-ed' to indicate past tense is an example of a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Derivational morpheme\",\n      \"Inflectional morpheme\",\n      \"Prefix\",\n      \"Suffix root\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Inflectional morpheme\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Derivational morphemes change word class or meaning.\",\n      \"Correct! Inflectional morphemes mark grammar (tense, number, comparison) without changing word class.\",\n      \"Prefixes attach at the beginning.\",\n      \"This is an inflectional suffix, not a root.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q319\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Students comparing English and Spanish word endings practice:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Syntax\",\n      \"Metalinguistic awareness\",\n      \"Fluency\",\n      \"Phonology\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Metalinguistic awareness\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not word analysis.\",\n      \"Correct! Metalinguistic awareness is consciously thinking about language structures.\",\n      \"Fluency is speed and accuracy, not language analysis.\",\n      \"Phonology is sound systems, not morphology.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q320\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"A classroom focused on conversation, gestures, and visual aids demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Grammar-Translation\",\n      \"Communicative approach\",\n      \"Audio-lingual method\",\n      \"Direct method\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Communicative approach\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Grammar-Translation emphasizes written translation and explicit grammar.\",\n      \"Correct! The Communicative approach emphasizes meaningful interaction and context.\",\n      \"Audio-lingual method focuses on drills and repetition.\",\n      \"Direct method uses target language only, without emphasizing visuals/gestures as much.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q321\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Teachers lower the affective filter by:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Increasing test frequency\",\n      \"Creating a supportive environment\",\n      \"Correcting every error publicly\",\n      \"Reducing peer interaction\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Creating a supportive environment\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Frequent testing can raise anxiety.\",\n      \"Correct! A supportive, low-anxiety environment lowers the affective filter and promotes acquisition.\",\n      \"Public correction raises anxiety.\",\n      \"Peer interaction supports language development.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q322\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Providing sentence frames such as 'I think ___ because ___' helps students:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Build syntax and academic discourse\",\n      \"Practice phonics\",\n      \"Drill pronunciation\",\n      \"Avoid code-switching\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Build syntax and academic discourse\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Sentence frames scaffold academic language structure and reasoning.\",\n      \"Phonics involves sound-letter relationships, not sentence structure.\",\n      \"Pronunciation is separate from sentence structure.\",\n      \"Sentence frames support language use, not eliminate code-switching.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q323\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"When teachers explicitly discuss word roots and affixes, they develop:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Morphological awareness\",\n      \"Phonemic fluency\",\n      \"Syntactic transfer\",\n      \"Listening comprehension\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Morphological awareness\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Discussing roots and affixes builds morphological awareness—understanding word structure.\",\n      \"Phonemic fluency involves sound manipulation, not word parts.\",\n      \"Syntactic transfer refers to sentence structure influence from L1.\",\n      \"Listening comprehension requires different strategies.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q324\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Suffixes that form nouns often end in:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"-able, -ous\",\n      \"-ize, -ify\",\n      \"-ness, -tion\",\n      \"-ed, -ing\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"-ness, -tion\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"-able and -ous form adjectives.\",\n      \"-ize and -ify form verbs.\",\n      \"Correct! -ness and -tion are common noun-forming suffixes.\",\n      \"-ed and -ing are inflectional suffixes.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q325\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"The best way to help ELLs develop academic vocabulary is to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Give bilingual dictionaries\",\n      \"Use context-rich content lessons\",\n      \"Simplify all reading\",\n      \"Limit talk time\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Use context-rich content lessons\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Dictionaries are helpful but don't build deep understanding.\",\n      \"Correct! Context-rich lessons provide meaningful exposure and use of academic vocabulary.\",\n      \"Simplifying prevents exposure to academic language.\",\n      \"Talk time is essential for language development.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q326\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which era of English followed the Norman Conquest and saw major French influence?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Old English\",\n      \"Middle English\",\n      \"Modern English\",\n      \"Contemporary English\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Middle English\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Old English (450-1100) predates the Norman Conquest.\",\n      \"Correct! Middle English (1100-1500) developed after the Norman Conquest brought French vocabulary into English.\",\n      \"Modern English came later, from 1500 onward.\",\n      \"Contemporary English is not a standard periodization term.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q327\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Hard\",\n    \"question\": \"Which event greatly affected vowel pronunciation between 1400–1600?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The Great Schism\",\n      \"Industrial Revolution\",\n      \"Great Vowel Shift\",\n      \"Norman Conquest\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Great Vowel Shift\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"The Great Schism was a religious division, not a linguistic change.\",\n      \"The Industrial Revolution occurred later (1760-1840) and affected society, not pronunciation.\",\n      \"Correct! The Great Vowel Shift changed how long vowels were pronounced, creating spelling-pronunciation mismatches.\",\n      \"The Norman Conquest (1066) affected vocabulary but not this specific vowel change.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q328\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"The Renaissance and Shakespeare contributed to the rise of:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Old English\",\n      \"Early Modern English\",\n      \"Middle English\",\n      \"Contemporary English\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Early Modern English\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Old English ended around 1100 CE.\",\n      \"Correct! Early Modern English (1500-1800) includes the Renaissance, Shakespeare, and early standardization.\",\n      \"Middle English ended around 1500 CE.\",\n      \"Contemporary English came later.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q329\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"A word adopted directly from another language, such as 'ballet', is called a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Calque\",\n      \"Loanword\",\n      \"Creole\",\n      \"Derivation\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Loanword\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Calques translate structure, not adopt words directly.\",\n      \"Correct! A loanword is borrowed unchanged from another language (ballet, piano, café).\",\n      \"Creole is a type of language, not a borrowed word.\",\n      \"Derivation creates new words from existing roots, not borrowing.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q330\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"A word-for-word translation of a foreign phrase is known as a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Pidgin\",\n      \"Calque\",\n      \"Borrowing\",\n      \"Neologism\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Calque\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Pidgin is a simplified contact language, not a translation method.\",\n      \"Correct! A calque translates the structure literally (skyscraper = gratte-ciel).\",\n      \"Borrowing is general; calque is specifically literal translation.\",\n      \"Neologism is a newly coined word.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q331\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"The study of word origins and their historical changes is called:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Etymology\",\n      \"Morphology\",\n      \"Phonology\",\n      \"Syntax\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Etymology\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Etymology studies word origins and semantic evolution.\",\n      \"Morphology studies word structure.\",\n      \"Phonology studies sound systems.\",\n      \"Syntax studies sentence structure.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q332\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"A simplified communication system between speakers of different languages is called a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Pidgin\",\n      \"Creole\",\n      \"Dialect\",\n      \"Sociolect\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Pidgin\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Pidgins are simplified contact languages with no native speakers.\",\n      \"Creoles evolve from pidgins when they gain native speakers.\",\n      \"Dialects are varieties of a single language.\",\n      \"Sociolects are social varieties.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q333\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"A stable, fully developed language that evolved from a pidgin is known as a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Dialect\",\n      \"Creole\",\n      \"Idiolect\",\n      \"Sociolect\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Creole\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Dialects are regional/social varieties, not evolved pidgins.\",\n      \"Correct! Creoles develop when pidgins gain native speakers and expand grammatically.\",\n      \"Idiolects are individual speech patterns.\",\n      \"Sociolects are social group varieties.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q334\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Which of the following best defines 'dialect'?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"A social language register\",\n      \"A simplified communication form\",\n      \"A regional or social language variety\",\n      \"A new language created from two others\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"A regional or social language variety\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Register refers to formality level, not dialect.\",\n      \"This describes pidgin, not dialect.\",\n      \"Correct! Dialects are systematic regional or social varieties of a language.\",\n      \"This describes a creole or mixed language.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q335\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"An individual's unique way of speaking is referred to as their:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Sociolect\",\n      \"Dialect\",\n      \"Idiolect\",\n      \"Register\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Idiolect\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Sociolect is group-based, not individual.\",\n      \"Dialect is regional/social, not individual.\",\n      \"Correct! Idiolect is an individual's unique linguistic fingerprint.\",\n      \"Register is formality level, not individual style.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q336\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Language associated with specific social groups or classes is called a:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Pidgin\",\n      \"Sociolect\",\n      \"Dialect\",\n      \"Register\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Sociolect\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Pidgin is a contact language, not a social variety.\",\n      \"Correct! Sociolect reflects social class, education, or group membership.\",\n      \"Dialect is regional, not specifically social.\",\n      \"Register is formality level.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q337\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"Which statement best defines slang?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"A fixed formal register\",\n      \"A type of professional jargon\",\n      \"Informal language used for identity or novelty\",\n      \"Words borrowed from Latin\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Informal language used for identity or novelty\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Slang is informal, not formal.\",\n      \"Jargon is specialized; slang is broader and more informal.\",\n      \"Correct! Slang is informal, group-specific, and evolves rapidly.\",\n      \"Latin borrowings are not necessarily slang.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q338\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Easy\",\n    \"question\": \"The phrase 'cool' meaning 'good' or 'stylish' is an example of:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"A regionalism\",\n      \"A neologism\",\n      \"Slang\",\n      \"Borrowing\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Slang\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Not limited to one region.\",\n      \"It's not newly coined, but repurposed.\",\n      \"Correct! 'Cool' for 'good' is informal slang that emerged mid-20th century.\",\n      \"It's not borrowed from another language.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q339\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"The word 'rodeo' entered English from:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"German\",\n      \"Spanish\",\n      \"French\",\n      \"Italian\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Spanish\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"German did not contribute 'rodeo.'\",\n      \"Correct! 'Rodeo' comes from Spanish, reflecting cultural exchange in the American West.\",\n      \"Not from French.\",\n      \"Not from Italian.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q340\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Variation between 'pop' and 'soda' across U.S. regions demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Morphology\",\n      \"Lexical variation\",\n      \"Phonemic drift\",\n      \"Idiolect\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Lexical variation\",\n    