glish idioms with ones in their native language practice:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Syntax transfer\",\n      \"Cross-linguistic connections\",\n      \"Phonemic decoding\",\n      \"Rote learning\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Cross-linguistic connections\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Syntax transfer relates to sentence structure, not vocabulary comparison.\",\n      \"Correct! Cross-linguistic connections leverage knowledge across languages to deepen understanding.\",\n      \"Phonemic decoding is sound-letter relationships, not idiom comparison.\",\n      \"Rote learning is memorization without understanding.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q218\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"Which instructional practice best develops metacognitive reading skills?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Silent reading logs\",\n      \"Think-aloud modeling\",\n      \"Dictation practice\",\n      \"Listening tests\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Think-aloud modeling\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Silent reading logs track reading but don't model metacognition.\",\n      \"Correct! Think-aloud modeling explicitly demonstrates comprehension monitoring and strategy use.\",\n      \"Dictation doesn't develop metacognitive awareness.\",\n      \"Listening tests assess, not develop, metacognition.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q219\",\n    \"type\": \"mcq\",\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"Medium\",\n    \"question\": \"A student writes 'She no like apples.' This shows:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Code-switching\",\n      \"Negative transfer\",\n      \"Fossilization\",\n      \"Overgeneralization\"\n    ],\n    \"answer\": \"Negative transfer\",\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Code-switching alternates complete languages, not creates errors.\",\n      \"Correct! Negative